   - the processing placement's `Status` is `suspended`
   - `ListRepos` itself errors
   - `ListClusters` errors, or no cluster matches the placement's slug
5. Same unresolved cases for `resolveRepoCellPlacement` (it likely shares the `resolveProcessingPlacement` helper, so this may be largely the same fixtures reused — don't skip writing the actual assertions, but DRY the fixture construction with a helper).
6. `TestResolveRepoCellTarget_ULID*` tests: update to the new `(target, error)` return signature. Keep the ULID behavior semantics intact (`GetRepo` still used, `cellTargetForClusterHost`/`matchClusterByHost` still used) — a `GetRepo` failure is now a loud error too (it always was repo-scoped), so rename `TestResolveRepoCellTarget_ULIDError_FallsBack` to something like `TestResolveRepoCellTarget_ULIDError_ReturnsError` and assert `err != nil` instead of `target == nil`.
7. `TestResolveRepoCellTarget_JurisdictionLowercased` and `TestResolveRepoCellTarget_NoClusterMatch_FallsBack` (rename the latter similarly) — update signatures/assertions; the lowercasing behavior itself (both host-path and slug-path) must still work, so mirror the jurisdiction-lowercasing test for the slug path too if `cellTargetForClusterSlug` duplicates that logic (it should, matching `cellTargetForClusterHost`'s existing normalization).
8. Keep `TestResolveRepoCellPlacement_BoundedByDeadline` passing with the blocking now on `ListRepos` — update the fake wiring, keep the assertions (timeout error, wraps `context.DeadlineExceeded`, bounded wait).
9. Keep `TestRequiredCellResolveTimeoutIsItsOwnBudget` — still valid, no change needed (still true that `requiredCellResolveTimeout > cellResolveTimeout`).
10. `TestResolveRepoCellPlacement_PairsIDWithItsOwnCell`, `_MultiRegionPicksAPlacement`, `_SkipsInactiveAndUnplaceable`, `_NoMirror` — these test mirror-based `resolveRepoCellPlacement` semantics that no longer apply verbatim (there's no more "pick first active mirror" or "skip inactive mirrors in a loop" — there's exactly one processing placement, found or not). Rewrite them against the new processing-placement semantics: e.g. `_SkipsInactiveAndUnplaceable` becomes "processing placement itself is failed/suspended → error" (already covered by case 4 above) rather than "skip a bad one and use another" (that scenario no longer exists — there is no fallback placement to skip to). Don't force the old test names/scenarios to survive if the underlying behavior they tested no longer exists; replace with tests that describe the actual new contract, and say so via the test name/doc comment.

Do not leave the `mirrors`/`mirrorsErr` fields on `fakeCellCore` if truly nothing in this file uses them anymore after your rewrite — check before deleting (this test file is `cell_target_test.go` specifically; other test files with their own fakes are out of scope).

## Verification (do ALL of this before reporting done — do not claim success without it)

1. `mise run fmt && mise run lint` from the worktree root — fix anything either flags. If fmt changes files, rerun lint on the formatted tree.
2. `mise run test` (or `go test ./cmd/entire/cli/...` for a faster inner loop while iterating) — all tests pass, including the full existing `cell_target_test.go` suite and any other package that references these functions/types (search `rg -n "resolveRepoCellTarget|resolveRepoCellPlacement|NewAuthenticatedEntireAPICellClient" --type go` across the whole repo once you're done, and fix every remaining reference/caller/test, including any I haven't listed here — I did a scoped search but you must re-verify against the final diff).
3. Confirm your new regression test (#1 above) actually fails against the pre-fix code: `git stash`, run just that test, confirm it fails with the old behavior, `git stash pop`, confirm it passes now. Report this explicitly.
4. `mise run test:ci` if time permits (full CI-equivalent suite) — at minimum run the unit test suite for the affected packages plus a full `mise run test`.
5. Manual proof against **prod** (read-only, safe): from the worktree, `go run ./cmd/entire trail list --repo gh/entirehq/entire.io` and paste the actual output. It must list real trails (or a clean empty result) sourced from `aws-us-east-2`, not the old "No open trails found." silent-wrong-region failure. If this requires being logged in and you are not, say so explicitly rather than skipping silently — do not fabricate output.
6. Check the branch size: `git diff --numstat origin/main...HEAD` — this should land comfortably under the ~400 net line / ~10 file soft ceiling for a PR. Report the actual numbers.

## Scope boundaries — do NOT do these

- Do NOT touch user-scoped `/me` routing (`activity`, `recap`, `auth.NewEntireAPICellClient(ctx, insecureHTTP, nil)` call sites) — those are correct as-is and out of scope.
- Do NOT touch `cell_fanout.go`'s repo-set fan-out logic (`groupReposByCell`, `resolveCellBaseURLs`, `fanOutCells`) — separate routing shape, out of scope, though it references `cellResolveTimeout`/`resolveRepoCellTarget` in comments only.
- Do NOT add caching, a BFF fallback, or region-guessing heuristics. Smallest change that makes multi-homed repos resolve correctly via the processing placement while single-homed repos keep working exactly as before.
- Do NOT modify `explain_repo.go` beyond what's needed to keep it compiling against `resolveRepoCellPlacement`'s (unchanged) `(repoCellPlacement, error)` signature — it already propagates errors correctly.
- Do NOT create a new git worktree — use the one already at /Users/alex/workspace/cli/.worktrees/fix-repo-cell-processing-placement.
- Do NOT commit or push. Leave the changes staged/unstaged in the worktree for review. (If you want to make an initial commit to checkpoint TDD progress that's fine, but do not push.)

## What to report back

- A summary of every file changed and why.
- Full test output showing the new/updated tests pass.
- The "does the regression test fail on pre-fix code" verification result (explicit before/after).
- The `git diff --numstat origin/main...HEAD` line/file counts.
- The manual `trail list --repo gh/entirehq/entire.io` output (or an explicit note that you couldn't run it and why — e.g. not logged in).
- Any place you deviated from this brief and why (e.g. if a coreapi type doesn't match what's described here exactly — re-verify field names/types against the actual generated code in `internal/coreapi` before writing code, since this brief was written from research notes, not by reading the generated file yourself).

