## Background

PR #2046 fixed `resolveRepoCellTarget`/`resolveRepoCellPlacement` (`cmd/entire/cli/cell_target.go`) to route repo-scoped reads (trails, experts) to the correct entire-api cell via the repo's processing placement, instead of silently falling back to the wrong region. That PR's `api_client.go` doc comment explains why this matters: "[NewAuthenticatedEntireAPICellClient] exchanges the login JWT for a jurisdictional identity token and dials the entire-api cell directly, because the BFF does not proxy these routes for bearer callers (COR-666)." In other words, `/api/v1/trails/...` is NOT served correctly through the generic data-API/BFF client (`NewAuthenticatedAPIClient`, which targets `api.BaseURL()`) for CLI bearer-token callers — only through the cell-routed client.

Two more call sites independently build a client via `NewAuthenticatedAPIClient` and then call `client.TrailsEnabled(...)` on it — the exact same bug class, just via a different (non-trail-command) code path. Both are now fixable cleanly using PR #2046's cell-routing machinery, which is why this PR stacks on top of it rather than off main.

## Fix 1 — `cmd/entire/cli/agent_help_cmd.go:352`

```go
// refreshAgentHelpTrailsEnabledCacheIfStaleForScope refreshes synchronously
// because agent-help is an explicit command whose output must reflect the
// current availability decision. SessionStart uses the detached
// refreshTrailsEnabledCacheIfStaleForScope path instead to avoid hook latency.
func refreshAgentHelpTrailsEnabledCacheIfStaleForScope(ctx context.Context, scope trailEnablementScope) error {
	if cachedTrailsEnablementForScope(ctx, scope, time.Now()) != trailEnablementCacheUnknown {
		return nil
	}
	if !scope.Supported {
		return saveTrailsEnabledForScope(ctx, scope, false, time.Now())
	}
	client, err := NewAuthenticatedAPIClient(ctx, false)   // <-- BUG: wrong client (BFF, not the repo's cell)
	if err != nil {
		return err
	}
	_, err = refreshTrailsEnabledCacheForScope(ctx, client, scope)
	return err
}
```

**Fix**: replace the `NewAuthenticatedAPIClient(ctx, false)` call with `trailRefreshAPIClient(ctx, false, scope.Owner+"/"+scope.Repo)` — the existing package-level seam already defined in `cmd/entire/cli/trail_context_cache.go`:
```go
var trailRefreshAPIClient = func(ctx context.Context, insecureHTTP bool, fullName string) (*api.Client, error) {
	return newTrailAPIClient(ctx, insecureHTTP, fullName)
}
```
This is the exact same seam `runTrailEnablementRefresh` already uses correctly (`trail_context_cache.go:298`: `trailRefreshAPIClient(ctx, false, scope.Owner+"/"+scope.Repo)`) — reuse it verbatim rather than inventing a new one, and update its doc comment (currently says "the authenticated-client seam used by runTrailEnablementRefresh") to name both consumers now.

No other change needed in this function — `agentHelpRepoContextWithRefresh` (the caller, unchanged) already handles a refresh error gracefully (short backoff, returns `(repoLine, false)`), so a resolution failure from the now-loud cell client degrades exactly the same way a BFF-client failure used to.

**Test** (TDD, write first — this function currently has NO direct test; existing tests in `cmd/entire/cli/agent_help_cmd_test.go` inject a fake `refresh` closure and bypass it entirely): add a new test that swaps the package-level `trailRefreshAPIClient` var to record the arguments it was called with and return a synthetic error (avoiding any real HTTP/auth), then calls `refreshAgentHelpTrailsEnabledCacheIfStaleForScope(ctx, scope)` directly with a `trailEnablementScope{Owner: "acme", Repo: "widget", Forge: "gh", Supported: true, ...}` (check `trailEnablementScope`'s full field list and what else is required — e.g. `RepoKey`, `APIBase`, `AuthKey` — before constructing the fixture; look at how other tests in this package build one, e.g. in `lifecycle_test.go` or `agent_help_cmd_test.go`). Assert:
